window.LyraDocsIndex = [{"title": "Getting started", "description": "Learn about the editions, check the requirements and prepare for your first contact with Lyra OS.", "url": "primeiros-passos.html", "text": "Lyra OS 16.1 is in development. Alpha and Beta images are test releases. Start in a virtual machine or on a dedicated computer and keep a copy of your files. Choose your edition Lyra OS is a system based on openSUSE Leap, with its own tools to organize the user and administration experience. The Lyra version is independent from the version of its base. Desktop: for personal computers. This guide follows the GNOME edition and the Vega control center. Server: for services and infrastructure, without a graphical environment. Administration uses the console, Vega CLI and Vega Web. Use the downloads section to choose the published image. The Desktop and Server cycles are independent; do not expect the same development stage in both editions. Check the requirements See the Desktop and Server requirements table before preparing the machine. Set aside a dedicated disk, UEFI boot and network access for updates. Try it before installing In a virtual machine, use UEFI firmware and a dedicated virtual disk. On the Desktop, the live session lets you check video, keyboard, mouse, audio and network before proceeding. Write down anything that does not work so you can include it in a problem report. The documented installation flow uses the entire disk. To prepare the media and review the disk precautions, follow Install Lyra OS . After the first login Confirm language, keyboard, time zone and network connection. Open Vega from the applications menu and check the system summary. Look up the available updates and read the changes before confirming. Set up a backup routine for your files on another device. Continue with Meet Vega or with the Server guide ."}, {"title": "Install Lyra OS", "description": "From ISO download to first boot: integrity, installation media and target disk.", "url": "instalacao.html", "text": "1. Prepare the computer Check the edition requirements , a network connection and a USB drive large enough for the ISO. A virtual machine with a dedicated disk also works for testing. A whole-disk installation erases the data on the selected disk. Back up before you start and check the model and capacity of the target. The USB drive will also be erased when the image is written. This guide covers the Desktop with GNOME. For the console installer and the storage limits of the other edition, see Install the Server . Do not use this procedure as instructions for dual boot or manual partitioning. 2. Download and verify the ISO Go to the website's downloads section . Download the ISO and the matching checksum file from the same release directory on SourceForge. On Linux, open a terminal in the folder containing both files. Example for the Desktop 16.1 RC 2 image: sha256sum -c LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 gpg --verify LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256.asc LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 The name must match the downloaded file exactly. Continue only if the check reports OK . If there is a mismatch, download the files again. The checksum verifies integrity; the .asc signature of the checksum confirms its origin. First import the official release key (fingerprint 01B6 3EED BE6B 0791 26A0 116E FA73 53A1 31EC EFEB ); the result must be a good signature from that key. 3. Write the media and boot in UEFI mode Open a disk image writing tool. In GNOME Disks, select the USB drive and use the option to restore a disk image. Select the ISO and double-check the target device before confirming. Copying the ISO file into a folder on the USB drive does not replace writing the image. Wait for the write to finish, eject the media and restart the computer. Open the firmware boot menu and select the media's UEFI entry. The key varies by manufacturer. In a virtual machine, attach the ISO as an optical drive and enable UEFI. Secure Boot is part of the flow planned by the project; record any boot failure for investigation. 4. Follow the Lyra Installer Wait for the live session and for the Lyra Installer to open. Fill in the language, keyboard, region and account options presented by the installer. Choose the disk dedicated to the installation and review the summary, especially the target and the storage changes. Confirm only after reviewing the data. Keep the computer on until the installation completes. Restart when prompted and remove the media to boot from the installed disk. The screens may change between candidates. 5. Check the installed system Log in with the account you created, test the network and open Vega. Follow the first steps after installation . If the installer fails, write down the step and the message before restarting; see how to report the problem . Reference: Desktop 16.1 RC 2 release notes (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Lyra OS Server", "description": "Install the server edition and start managing it from the console with Vega CLI.", "url": "server.html", "text": "An edition for service administration Lyra OS Server is an edition without a graphical environment. It is administered through the console, Vega CLI and Vega Web. The 16.1 RC 3 candidate uses the openSUSE Leap 16.1 base and brings health diagnostics, the weekly check and disk space cleanup to Vega CLI and Vega Web. Storage in the Server 16.1 series: whole-disk UEFI installation, with GPT, an EFI partition and an ext4 root. RAID, LVM, Btrfs, Snapper, legacy BIOS and unattended installation are outside the documented flow for this candidate. Install the Server Check the requirements , prepare a dedicated disk and back up. The contents of the chosen disk will be erased. Download the Server image from the downloads section and verify the integrity files from the same release. Write the ISO and boot in UEFI mode. The installer is interactive and runs in the console. Enter the language, keyboard, time zone, hostname and account details requested. Review the chosen disk and confirm the installation only after checking the summary. When it finishes, restart from the installed disk and log in with the account you created. Prepare Ethernet with DHCP for the initial setup. Network adjustments, such as a static address, are made after installation. Manage with Vega CLI On a local interactive console or in an already configured SSH session, run as your user: vega Use the menus to check the system state and manage the available functions. Authentication is requested for each privileged operation. Check the first boot These commands query the system state without changing the configuration: cat /etc/os-release ip -brief address systemctl --failed Check the installed version, the address received by the network interface and any failed services. The initial policy described by the project allows SSH and Vega Web on port 9090/tcp; confirm the actual state of the firewall and services on your installation before remote access. Vega Web is intended for administration on the local network. See its documentation to configure HTTPS access and authentication: Vega Web guide . Keep a recovery copy Plan backups of the data and of the service configurations. This edition does not offer Snapper rollback or automatic downgrade of an installation on the new base. See updates and troubleshooting . References: Server 16.1 RC 3 release notes and Server architecture (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Apps and updates", "description": "Manage packages and understand the difference between updating the system and upgrading to a new version.", "url": "atualizacoes.html", "text": "Install apps with Vega Open Vega and go to the software area. Search for the app, check its source and review the operation before confirming. Vega manages RPM packages via Zypper and Flatpak apps when that support is available. RPMs are part of the distribution's package system. Flatpaks use their own repositories and runtimes. The same app may appear from more than one source; choose the one you intend to keep to avoid duplicate installations. Update the installed system Connect the computer to the network and to a power source. Open the Vega updates area and refresh the list of available updates. Read the summary and confirm the changes you want. Wait for the result. Restart if the tool asks you to or if an update needs a new boot to take effect. If you prefer the terminal, the update flow for RPM packages is: sudo zypper refresh sudo zypper update The first command refreshes the repository metadata; the second presents the updates for confirmation. They do not update Flatpak apps. Do not run another package operation through Vega at the same time. Take care of package sources Keep the repositories that match the base of your installation. Do not add repositories from another Leap version to get a single package. If an unknown key or an unexpected change of source appears, check the project's announcement before accepting. Updating is not upgrading to a new version Changing the base requires a specific procedure. Updating packages does not mean you can migrate between openSUSE Leap versions or between Lyra candidates. Wait for a documented migration path for your source and target. The Alpha 7 notes state that migrating existing installations from the Leap 16.0 base to 16.1 depends on specific validation. Do not change the repository addresses or use a distribution upgrade as a shortcut. Backup and recovery Keep your important files on another device before major changes. Snapshots, when configured, help recover the system, but they do not replace an independent copy of the data. Server 16.1 uses ext4 and does not offer the Desktop's Snapper rollback flow. References: management with Vega and Alpha 7 migration limits (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Parental controls", "description": "Create a supervised account for a child, set the rules and learn the limits of the controls.", "url": "controle-parental.html", "text": "What it is Lyra OS parental controls are basic : they let a child use the computer in their own account, with rules set by a parent or guardian. They use the GNOME Parental Controls application and a supervised account created in Vega. Create the supervised account Open Vega and go to Users . Click New user (or Edit on an existing account) and turn on Supervised account . A supervised account is never an administrator. Review what supervision covers and does not cover, then save. Set the rules On the account card, click Parental Controls . The application opens for that account and asks for the administrator password. There you can restrict web browsers, block applications, prevent application installation and choose the age rating. The same rules appear in Settings → System → Users. What the supervised account cannot do Administer the system or install software system-wide: all of that asks for an administrator password. Log in on the text console or over SSH, or run commands with Alt+F2. See the terminals or Vega in the menus and search. Change or remove the rules set by the parent. Limits The controls do not resist a tech-savvy child: shortcuts, scripts or programs they create or download can still run. Blocked browsers and applications disappear from the menus, but remain installed on the system. The controls do not filter websites or keep browsing history. Anyone with the administrator password, a bootable USB drive or access to the disk can undo the rules. Flatpak Flatpak is not installed by default. To use Flathub applications, open Vega in Software and click Enable Flatpak . With Flatpak enabled, a tech-savvy child can install Flatpak applications in their own account and open blocked Flatpak applications; Vega warns about this before enabling it. The other rules still apply. References: GNOME help on parental controls and ADR 0019 ."}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "description": "Investigate boot, network and update failures and prepare a useful report for the project.", "url": "solucao-de-problemas.html", "text": "The media does not boot Check the ISO checksum as described in the installation guide . Confirm that the image was written to the USB drive and that the firmware is booting from the correct UEFI entry. In a VM, check UEFI, memory and that the ISO is attached to the optical drive. Record the message shown, the machine model and the Secure Boot state. No network connection Check the cable or the Wi-Fi connection in the settings. On the Server, confirm that the network provides DHCP. To look up the interface addresses: ip -brief address If there is an address but the repositories do not respond, note whether the error mentions DNS, connection or certificate. Also check the system date and time. This information helps distinguish network failures from an unavailable repository. An update failed Wait for other package operations to finish and try again. Do not delete lock files or force the package manager to quit. Copy the error message and the name of the repository involved. Read the guidance on package sources before accepting a new key, removing repositories or changing the base. Gather system information To identify the version, the kernel and failed services, run: cat /etc/os-release uname -r systemctl --failed If your image includes lyra-report , run it as a regular user: lyra-report The tool shows the collected data for review before creating a local file and does not upload anything automatically. If the command is not available, use the basic information above; you do not need to install development components to report a failure. Report a reproducible problem With Vega, the quickest way is the Ask for help button in the diagnostics: see Ask for help . Open an issue in the corresponding repository: Desktop , Server or Vega . Search for a similar report first. Include the information below: Lyra OS edition and version: ISO name, if the failure happened during installation: Computer model or VM configuration: UEFI and Secure Boot state: Steps to reproduce: Expected result: Observed result and error message: Review screenshots and logs before publishing. Remove passwords, tokens, keys and personal data, even when the tool applies anonymization. Share only the files needed to explain the problem. Reference: diagnostics with lyra-report ."}, {"title": "Ask for help", "description": "Put together a help request from the Vega diagnostics, without personal data, and post it in the forum or an issue.", "url": "pedir-ajuda.html", "text": "Before asking Many problems can be solved in Vega itself. In Hardware → Health diagnostics , run the Quick diagnostics: each item in Attention or Failure shows what to do and, when there is a safe fix, the Fix button. For connection problems, use Network → My internet does not work , which shows at which step the connection stopped. Ask for help from Vega Run the diagnostics in Hardware → Health diagnostics and open View report . Click Ask for help . In What is happening? , describe the problem: what you did, what you expected and what happened. Review the text that will be shared. You can edit everything before sending. Pick a destination: Open in the forum , Open a GitHub issue or Copy , to paste wherever you like. The browser opens the page already filled in. Nothing is published until you confirm on the site itself. What is shared The text includes your description, the computer model, the versions of the system, kernel, vegad and Vega, the result of each test and the attention and failure findings, with the raw data shortened. It fits in 6,000 characters. Disk serial numbers, MAC addresses, the Wi-Fi network name, the computer name and account names appear as [redacted] . Even so, read the text before posting: the forum and issues are public. Where to ask Questions and answers forum : questions and usage problems. Someone from the community or the project answers there. Desktop issues : a defect you can reproduce. Search for a similar report first. For the Server edition, use the Server issues and the information from Gather system information . Without Vega If Vega does not open, follow Report a reproducible problem and paste the output of the commands listed there."}, {"title": "Requirements and technical terms", "description": "Look up the hardware requirements and the meaning of the terms used in the guides.", "url": "referencia.html", "text": "Check the requirements The values below are provisional and follow the requirements published on the website. Apps and services may need more resources. Minimum and recommended configuration Resource Desktop Server Minimum processor x86_64, 2 cores x86_64, 1 core Minimum memory 4 GiB 2 GiB Minimum disk 24 GiB 8 GiB Recommended 4 cores, 8 GiB of RAM, 40 GiB SSD or larger 2 or more cores, 4 GiB of RAM or more, 20 GiB SSD or larger Set up UEFI boot. On the Desktop, the minimum screen resolution is 1024 × 768, with Full HD recommended. On the Server, prepare an Ethernet connection with DHCP. Make sure you have internet access to get updates. Terms used in these guides ISO and live session An ISO is the image used to create the boot media. The live session runs the system from that media before installation. UEFI and Secure Boot UEFI is the firmware used in the documented boot flow. Secure Boot verifies the signatures of boot components according to the firmware's trust configuration. RPM and Zypper RPM is the system's package format. Zypper manages these packages and their repositories. Flatpak An app distribution format and tool with its own repositories and runtimes. Snapshot and backup A snapshot records a state of the file system. A backup is a copy of the data for recovery; an independent copy also protects against failure of the original disk. SHA-256 checksum A value used to check whether a file matches the expected content. It must be obtained from the release that corresponds to the image. Identify your installation cat /etc/os-release uname -r The first command shows the system identification. The second reports the running kernel. Also include the ISO name when reporting installation problems. References: requirements published on the website , Desktop 16.1 RC 2 and Server Beta 1.1 (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Meet Vega", "description": "Find software, network, hardware and service settings in the control center.", "url": "vega.html", "text": "Open the control center Vega brings together Lyra OS administration tasks. On the Desktop, look for Vega in the applications menu and open it with your regular account. Administrative authentication is requested when an operation needs authorization. GNOME Settings remains available for desktop preferences. Vega complements those options with management of packages, services and other system features. Where to find each task Dashboard: summary of the system's health and shortcuts. Software: packages, Flatpak apps, repositories and updates. Hardware and system: device inventory, kernel, storage, date and time. Network: connections, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, firewall, VPN and proxy, depending on the available components. Administration: users, services, logs and processes. Backup: features that depend on the installed tools and storage configuration. Screen names and availability may vary with the installed version. Optional features may appear unavailable when a dependency is missing. Before confirming an operation Read the action summary and check what will be installed, removed or changed. Wait for the operation to finish before starting another package management task. If an error occurs, record the full message and the chosen action. To manage programs and keep the system up to date, follow Apps and updates . Vega in the terminal On the Server with Vega CLI installed, open an interactive terminal with your account and run: vega The terminal interface shares the administration service with the other Vega interfaces. Queries do not require running the whole application as root; privileged actions request authentication. See also the Server guide . References: Vega documentation and Vega CLI ."}, {"title": "Versions and support", "description": "Understand Alpha, Beta, RC, Lyra's version numbers and how they relate to the openSUSE base.", "url": "versoes.html", "text": "Lyra's version follows openSUSE Leap The version uses the MAJOR.MINOR[.PATCH] format: MAJOR.MINOR is the version of the openSUSE Leap base and PATCH is a maintenance revision of Lyra itself. The 16.1 line is in development. The openSUSE Leap base determines the Lyra OS number: Lyra OS 16.1 is built on Leap 16.1. Until October 2026 the cycle was numbered 1.1; images already published under that number remain valid as a historical record. Alpha, Beta and RC Alpha: development candidate for testing integration and finding problems. Beta: validation and stabilization stage, still before the final release. RC: release candidate, subject to approval against the quality criteria. Identifiers such as 16.1-rc.2 distinguish the candidates; earlier images use 1.1-alpha.8 and 1.1-beta.1.1 . Desktop and Server have independent cycles. Moving to the next stage depends on testing and on resolving blockers, not just on a date. Check the notes for your image Always consult the release that corresponds to the file you downloaded. These are the documents for the images referenced in this edition of the guides: Desktop 16.1 RC 2 : second Desktop release candidate, with basic parental controls, a sudo security fix and Vega fixes; Flatpak and LinuxToys are no longer preinstalled. Desktop 16.1 RC 1 : first Desktop release candidate on Leap 16.1, with a signed checksum; strict freeze, fixes only until the stable release. Desktop 1.1 Alpha 8 : last Alpha of Desktop 16.1; new installations only, virtual-machine qualification and installer and dock fixes. Desktop 1.1 Alpha 7 : move of the base to Leap 16.1 and installation and upgrade limits. Server 16.1 RC 3 : third and final release candidate, frozen; with no P0/P1, the same ISO becomes the stable 16.1 release on 2026-11-19. It brings health diagnostics, the weekly check and disk space cleanup to Vega CLI and Vega Web. Server 16.1 RC 2 : second release candidate on the Leap 16.1 RC base, with the Vega fixes for remote administration. Server 1.1 Beta 1.1 : base revision preserving the scope of Beta 1. Community support Lyra OS is an independent project and version 16.1 will have community support. No contractual timeframe or end-of-support date is promised without a formal policy. Support for the openSUSE base and the cycle of a Lyra version are separate matters. To ask for help, describe the version and the situation following the problem reporting guide . For details of the policy maintained on the website, see the versioning document (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Announcements", "description": "Announcements of published Lyra OS releases, newest first.", "url": "anuncios.html", "text": "2026-10-28 · Lyra OS 16.1 RC 2 “Odisseia” available for testing The second release candidate of Lyra OS Desktop 16.1, with GNOME on openSUSE Leap 16.1, is ready to download. The stable release is planned for 5 November 2026 . Until then, only bug fixes go in. Download from SourceForge · Release notes What's new since RC 1 Basic parental controls. Create a supervised account on Vega's Users page and set the rules in GNOME's “Parental Controls” app: browsers, applications, installation and age rating. The supervised account cannot administer the system and does not see the terminals or Vega. These controls are basic: they do not filter websites. Security. Only members of the wheel group can use sudo . Before, the installer let any account become root with its own password. RC 1 installs get the fix through updates. Vega 5.3.2 with a new interface and several fixes: firewall zone, Bluetooth without an adapter, “Update all” leaving packages behind, and network names with accents. Power and graphics. GNOME power profiles work ( tuned-ppd ), the Intel and AMD Vulkan drivers ship in the image, and the NVIDIA GPU powers down when idle on hybrid laptops. Live session. The screen no longer locks during installation. Leaner installation. Flatpak and LinuxToys are no longer preinstalled. To use Flatpak, enable it on Vega's Software page; Flathub comes with it. Known issue In the live session, installing programs through Vega asks for a password the live account does not have. Install the system first and the programs afterwards. The fix goes into the stable release. Verification SHA-256: 9592becd756cfec6d58f5589f2da0d560ac175051df26790c90b08308c238873 . The checksum is signed with the official release key, fingerprint 01B6 3EED BE6B 0791 26A0 116E FA73 53A1 31EC EFEB . gpg --verify LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256.asc LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 sha256sum -c LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 Test it and report what you find in the GitHub issues ."}, {"title": "Desktop 16.1 RC 2", "description": "What is new, limits, qualification and integrity of the GNOME Desktop candidate.", "url": "notas-desktop.html", "text": "Lyra OS Desktop 16.1 RC 2 “Odisseia” is the second release candidate of the GNOME Desktop on openSUSE Leap 16.1 . It gathers the fixes found after RC 1 and brings basic parental controls . Apart from that exception, approved by the maintainer, the strict freeze still applies: until the stable release, only fixes for bugs, crashes, security and critical performance are accepted. Publication Desktop 16.1 RC 2 Published 2026-10-28 File LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso SHA-256 9592becd756cfec6d58f5589f2da0d560ac175051df26790c90b08308c238873 Download releases/16.1/rc2/desktop/ on SourceForge Source code tag v16.1-rc.2 (commit f5d4dc6 ) Base openSUSE Leap 16.1, kernel 6.12.0-160100.7 Because the image content changed, RC 2 is a new candidate and went through the full gate again. RC 1 remains listed as history. Changes since RC 1 Basic parental controls: create a supervised account on the Users page of Vega and set the rules in the GNOME Parental Controls app: browsers, applications, software installation and age rating. The supervised account cannot administer the system, cannot log in through a TTY or SSH, cannot use Alt+F2 and does not see the terminals or Vega; the Sheliak menus and search follow the rules. See the parental controls guide. sudo security: only accounts in the wheel group can use sudo , with their own password. Previously, the installer let any account become root with its own password. Existing installations receive the fix through the normal update. Vega 5.3.2: the Vega interface was rebuilt with Tauri, with the same features as the GTK version. Firewall in Vega: the status shows the zone that actually filters the traffic ( public ), no longer the docker zone or an empty zone. The firewall rules did not change; only the display was wrong. Bluetooth without an adapter: on machines without Bluetooth, Vega immediately shows “No Bluetooth adapter found” instead of loading for a minute and ending in an error, and the service no longer shows up as a failure on the Dashboard. “Update all”: Vega no longer leaves behind updates that depend on a new package from another repository. Network names with accents: connection names with accented characters, such as the Portuguese “Conexão cabeada 1”, are shown correctly in Vega, and setting a static IP on them works again. Live session: the screen no longer locks after a few idle minutes; before, anyone who stepped away during the installation lost the session, because the live account has no password. The installed system keeps the default GNOME lock. Power profiles: the GNOME power profile selector is back, provided by tuned-ppd . Graphics and hybrid laptops: the image includes the Intel and AMD Vulkan drivers, and GTK 4 now renders on the integrated GPU. When the NVIDIA driver is installed through Vega on a hybrid laptop, the NVIDIA GPU powers off when idle (RTD3), which saves battery. Flatpak and LinuxToys: no longer preinstalled. Flatpak, with Flathub, is enabled on the Software page of Vega, which warns you when there are supervised accounts. LinuxToys remains available in the Lyra repository. Existing installations keep what they already have. On the first update: vegad 5.1.47 arrives through zypper or Vega and asks for the administrator password only once for a queue of Flatpaks, instead of once per application. Scope and limits New installations only. If you already use RC 1, you receive Vega, vegad, the sudo fix and parental controls through the normal update, without reinstalling. The other image changes (power profiles, Vulkan drivers, Flatpak and LinuxToys out of the default installation) do not arrive through updates. Parental controls are basic: they do not filter websites and do not withstand a tech-savvy child. With Flatpak enabled, the supervised account can install and open Flatpak applications. GNOME is the only Desktop edition; the KDE Plasma and XFCE editions were discontinued. RAID, LVM, manual partitioning and side-by-side installation remain outside the covered scenario. System languages: en-US , pt-BR and es-ES , with English as the fallback. How the RC was qualified The exact ISO went through the release gate again in a QEMU/KVM virtual machine: live session with and without network, UEFI installation with Secure Boot, legacy BIOS and UEFI without EFI variables, first boot, update with snapshot rollback, languages and a check of the OBS repositories. On the installed system, the firewall zone in Vega, the power profile selector, the sudo policy and parental controls were also checked. Every item passed, and the GO decision is published with the release. Known issue: in the live session, installing software through Vega asks for a password the live account does not have, and the installation does not happen. The Lyra installer and the installed system are not affected; the fix goes into the stable release. Risks: qualification was done in a virtual machine, and validation on physical hardware is still in progress; with NVIDIA cards, an external monitor may not wake up after suspend. Integrity Download the ISO, the .sha256 file and the .sha256.asc signature from the same folder and check them: sha256sum -c LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 gpg --verify LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256.asc LyraOS-Desktop-16.1-rc.2-x86_64.iso.sha256 The signature must be valid for the official release key , the same as RC 1, fingerprint 01B6 3EED BE6B 0791 26A0 116E FA73 53A1 31EC EFEB . The step-by-step procedure is in the installation guide . Stable release The stable 16.1 release will be rebuilt from the final openSUSE Leap 16.1 packages, released on November 3, 2026, with the same Lyra content as RC 2, and will go through a new gate before publication, planned for November 5, 2026 , with November 26 as the limit. Source: release notes in the Desktop repository (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Server 16.1 RC 3", "description": "Vega changes, schedule, limits and integrity of the Server candidate.", "url": "notas-server.html", "text": "Lyra OS Server 16.1 RC 3 “Delos” is the third and final release candidate of the Server on openSUSE Leap 16.1 : a headless edition with a console installer and administration through Vega. RC 3 is the final candidate and is frozen: with no P0 or P1 issues, the same ISO is promoted to the stable 16.1 release (tag server-v16.1 ) on 2026-11-19, without a rebuild. Publication Server 16.1 RC 3 Published 2026-10-30 File LyraOS-Server-16.1-rc.3-x86_64.iso SHA-256 1bcb55a7842487ee463bf40640b882f34dd00e1a9a1a31d539a8de39919b7a45 Download releases/16.1/rc3/server/ on SourceForge Source code tag server-v16.1-rc.3 (commit 9c49dba ) Base openSUSE Leap 16.1 Updates from the final Leap 16.1 arrive through the update repository after installation. Schedule Server 16.1 milestones Milestone Date Status RC 2 2026-10-22 published, replaced by RC 3 RC 3 2026-10-30 final candidate, frozen Stable 16.1 2026-11-19 the same RC 3 ISO, promoted without a rebuild What changed since RC 2 The image, the installer and the functional limits are those of RC 2. What changes are the Vega packages and the way the system identity is written. vegad 5.6.2: full health diagnostics (package integrity, disk health, logs, platform, security, boot time and network), with safe fixes for each finding; a weekly check ( vegad-health-check.timer , enabled at installation) that runs the light tests at low priority and only warns about what is new; Free up space (core dumps, old journal, zypper cache, unused Flatpak, old kernels and Snapper cleanup). Over SSH, members of the wheel group read the weekly check result, the space measurement and the network test without a password, as in the local session. vega-cli 5.2.0: on startup, it shows a summary of weekly check findings not yet seen; Hardware and Kernel → Health diagnostics (quick mode, single test, report, Fix, I've seen the logs and check frequency); Network and Firewall → My internet doesn't work ; Storage → Free up space . The polkit password prompt appears on a clean screen and allows 5 minutes to type. vega-web 5.2.0: Hardware and Kernel → Health diagnostics , with progress, a report with boot time by phase and the network step by step, Fix per finding, periodic check and the latest weekly check; Network and Firewall → My internet doesn't work , with a single password; Storage → Free up space , with cleanup progress; Ask for help from the report; and the periodic check status on the home dashboard. System identity: Lyra's /etc/os-release is now a file of its own, as on the Desktop. It used to be written through the Leap-release package link: package diagnostics reported a modified program and Fix reinstalled Leap-release , bringing back the openSUSE identity. Scope and limits No new application or architecture change was added to the image. The edition remains headless, with UEFI whole-disk installation (GPT, EFI partition and ext4 root), an administrative account with sudo , a locked root account, SSH, firewalld and Vega ( vegad , vega-cli and vega-web ). RAID, LVM, Btrfs, Snapper, legacy BIOS, unattended installation and upgrades between releases remain outside the 16.1 scope. How the RC was qualified The full Server gate was repeated on the exact ISO, with vegad 5.6.2, vega-cli 5.2.0 and vega-web 5.2.0 in the package inventory. In addition to the items from the previous RCs, the gate checks over SSH the health diagnostics, the weekly check summary, the network test and Free up space in vega-cli , the same functions in vega-web through the browser, and that rpm -V Leap-release reports nothing on the installed system. Testing on physical hardware remains a declared risk. Known issue An update of the Leap-release package itself can still replace the system identity with openSUSE's. The issue has P3 priority and is tracked in issue #38 . Integrity The published bundle includes the ISO, the package inventory, the KIWI verification, CycloneDX and SPDX SBOMs, the checksum and the detached signature. Check them: sha256sum -c LyraOS-Server-16.1-rc.3-x86_64.iso.sha256 gpg --verify LyraOS-Server-16.1-rc.3-x86_64.iso.sha256.asc LyraOS-Server-16.1-rc.3-x86_64.iso.sha256 The signature must be valid for the official release key , fingerprint 01B6 3EED BE6B 0791 26A0 116E FA73 53A1 31EC EFEB . Installation is described in Install the Server . Source: release notes in the Server repository (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Contribute to the documentation", "description": "Suggest corrections, report questions and help improve the guides for other people.", "url": "contribuir.html", "text": "Found a gap? The documentation follows the evolution of Lyra OS. A confusing step, a different message in your version or a missing topic are good opportunities to contribute. Open the website repository and use the issues area to propose a correction. Include the page, the passage, the Lyra version you used and the result you observed. For system defects, use the repositories listed in the troubleshooting guide . Propose a change Fork the website repository on GitHub and create a branch for the change. Edit the article content in docs/content/ . The catalog.json file organizes titles, descriptions and categories. Run python3 scripts/build-docs.py at the project root to generate the pages and the search index. Check the navigation, the instructions and the layout on a small screen. Open a pull request with the correction and explain which version you checked the procedure on. Write for the people who will use the system Start with the goal and the requirements of the procedure. Explain what commands do before asking people to run them. Highlight operations that erase data or change storage. Keep released features separate from plans and features in development. Include a reference to the release notes or to the component's documentation. Translate and update translations Portuguese is the source language of the guides. The English, Spanish and Italian versions live in docs/content/en/ , docs/content/es/ and docs/content/it/ , each with its own catalog.json . Keep the same file name, the same h2 heading IDs and the commands unchanged. When revising a guide in Portuguese, also update the translations or leave them flagged as outdated. In the translation catalog, source_reviewed records the review date of the Portuguese original used as the basis. When the original is newer, the translated page shows a notice linking to the Portuguese version."}];
